DATAMAP TUTORIAL
================
for: PSION System 3a/c/(Siena?)

Author: (C) Peter von Walter; 28.5.99
email: vwalter@physi.uni-heidelberg.de
www: http://pi.physi.uni-heidelberg.de/~vwalter/my/homepage.html


This short tutorial should help you to build your own maps and to understand some of the priciples of
DATAMAP.


Defining a new map:
-------------------
Propably the most challenging part is setting up a new map and to place the different graphic objects
on it:

- Create a new map file. You can do this either by Psion-N from the system or by starting with any
file and selecting MAP-NEW from the menu. Choose a name which is appropriate for your new map, e.g.
"MyWorld.map".

- The setup dialog for defining your map comes up.

- You can leave the Window Width; Height as proposed or change it later to your needs (See memory
considerations).

- Now you have to define the size (Width and Height) of your new map. If you want to use only one
underlying picture, use the dimensions (in pixels) of this. If you don't know it yet, leave it as
proposed (480;160 = screen size), you can change it later any time.

- You propably don't want to use pixel coordinates (system coordinates) in your map but real
geographical values like Latitude and Longitude (user coordinates). Therefore your map has to be
calibrated for this. The values 'Left;Top' are the user coordinates of the map point '0;0', the
values for 'Right; Bottom' are the coordinates for the map point 'Width; Height' (the lower right
corner of the map) respectively. If you dont't know the values yet, leave them again as proposed, you
can do the calibration with actual reference points more easily later on.

- Grid defines the step size for standard cursor movements. leave it at 10 for now.

- Cursor Offset defines size and shape of the location cursor. Experiment later for yourself and
leave it at -10 for now.

- Coord.Format defines how location information is shown and written to the database. Select
'Degree&Minutes_Geo'. The coordinates will come out like '10¡14.2N;4¡24.3E', which is the standard
Lat;Lon format.

- Default Data allows you to position the open dialog on your default file. Leave it as is and press
ENTER!

- Now you are presented with an empty map where you can place your graphic objects.


Place a picture on your map:
----------------------------
- Select from the Menu the command "Draw-Picture (Psion-Shift-P)".

- A small intermediate dialog allows you to choose either the builtin WORLD picture or any picture
you have in a separate file (FILE). For this purpose lets select the standard picture WORLD.

- The edit dialog for a new picture comes up. The header shows something like 'Picture 2/2 [Size]'.
This means that this graphical object is the second record of a total of 2. You should know, that
there is already a record (Type 0) in the file, which describes the map itself.
- Leave file name and ID as proposed!

- Select in 'at' the location for your picture. To automatically center a picture on your map, leave
'at' empty!

- Plane can be either "Black, Grey or Both" as wanted. Leave Mode at 'Set'. Remember that you always
can change all properties afterwards.

-After Enter your picture should appear at the selected location. You still can move it around with
the arrow keys to the final destination. Normally the cursor moves in grid incrementals (e.g. 10),
use Shift-Arrowkeys to do the fine tuning.

- Press Enter to reenter the edit dialog or Esc to leave the graphics mode.


Place a Text object on your map:
--------------------------------
Assume you want to label an object on your map:

- Use the arrow-keys to put the cursor near the location for some text.

- Select from the Menu the command "Draw-Text (Psion-Shift-T)".

- A dialog asks for the actual 'Text' (e.g. "Island Hawaii"), what 'Font' and in what 'Style' you
want to draw it. Select again the appropriate 'Plane' and the 'Mode'. The location ('at') is the
current location and can be fine tuned after Enter.

- After Enter move the text with the arrowkeys to the exact location.

- Press Esc to leave the graphics mode.


Place a Line on your map:
--------------------------------
Assume you want to draw a line:

- Use the arrow-keys to put the cursor to the approximate starting point of the line.

- Select from the Menu the command "Draw-Line (Psion-Shift-L)".

- A second blinking cursor for the selected starting point of the line appears. Move the crosshair
cursor to the approximate ending point of the line and press Enter.

- A definition dialog for the line comes up. Fill out in a similar way as above!

- After Enter you could move the line with the arrowkeys to the exact location.

- If you want to continue the line at the ending point, simply use "Draw-Line (Psion-Shift-L)" again
and select the new ending point of the appended line!  NOTE: You can bypass the definition dialog by
pressing Shift-Enter and continue in the drawing of a polygon!

- Press Esc to leave the graphics mode.

If you want to edit any graphic object, press Cntrl-Enter to enter the graphic mode and press
Cntrl-Enter  (or Shift-Cntrl-Enter) again to cycle through all objects on the map. Use the arrowkeys
to move it to a different place or use Enter to open the edit dialog for the object. Remember: You
can change any property of every object at any time!


